INT. DRIVE-THRU WEDDING CHAPEL - BOOTH - NIGHT

A glass booth the size of a phone box. Plastic flowers. A speaker, a menu board with "VOWS - $29" and "FLOWERS - $9," and a tiny neon cross.

PATSY LEUNG-ARCHER, 20s, sequined vest and veil, sits on a stool. She wipes down the counter in slow circles. The only sound is the neon's buzz.

Headlights sweep across her face. A car idles outside. A window rolls down. She slides the glass open and leans out.

PATSY
Welcome to Holy Smokes Chapel. Would you like to marry someone tonight?

Nobody answers. Just the engine.

PATSY (CONT'D)
Sir? Ma'am?

A hand appears. In it, a gun.

Patsy freezes. Her lips move without sound.

WENDELL ASHFORD-LOPEZ, 40s, pulls his head out of the car and steps up to the booth. Leather jacket, bloodshot eyes. The gun stays on her.

WENDELL
Open the register.

PATSY
We don't have a register. We have a tip jar.

WENDELL
Then open the tip jar.

PATSY
It's a mason jar.

WENDELL
Open it!

She does, hands shaking. A few crumpled bills. A rubber band. A condolence card.

WENDELL (CONT'D)
That's all?

PATSY
People mostly pay with cards.

He stares into the jar. The gun lowers. He laughs, ragged.

WENDELL
I've been planning this for a month.

PATSY
I can marry you. Twenty-nine dollars.

WENDELL
To who?

PATSY
Anyone you like. I'm very flexible.

He pockets the gun. Takes out a crumpled twenty and a ten. Pushes them through.

WENDELL
Keep the dollar.

PATSY
Congratulations.

She adjusts her veil and begins the vows.
